#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074634} :activity/id #uuid "4417fe29-8f51-5120-ac66-9aaeadadb6f3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508 :topic/id #uuid "a587db09-9578-5d61-b811-99a95c85061d" :topic/math-academy-id 553} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075034 :tutorial/content "We can find **equivalent ratios** by multiplying or dividing all numbers in a ratio by the same value. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following ratio:\n\n$$\n2:6\n$$\n\nIf we multiply *both* numbers by $5,$ for example, we get the ratio $10:30.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,6 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓\\times \\,5 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓\\times \\,5 &  \\\\ \\,10 &  & : &  & \\,30 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratios $2:6$ and $10:30$ are equivalent. \n\nSimilarly, if we take the ratio $2:6$ and divide *both* numbers by $2,$ we get $1:3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,6 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓÷\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratios  $2:6$ and  $1:3$ are also equivalent." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc2c9ce9-c2b0-5d68-81db-b8b0273407f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8203 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6aa20fa4-3163-5025-84f7-a1ddd4324cd0" :step/math-academy-id 186681 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074635}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075035 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the following two ratios are equivalent:\n\n![](images/c6/c673bfcd099ef612f48bd0da7f8535cb9406d23088392e056884fb9415bb5100.svg)\n\nWhat is the missing number?\n\nTo find a ratio equivalent to $3 : 5,$ we multiply or divide both numbers by the same value. \n\nIn this case, we need to get $12$ from ${\\color{blue}{3}},$ so we multiply ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ by $4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,5 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓\\times \\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓\\times \\,4 &  \\\\ 12 &  & : &  & \\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{0}[/math]}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to get ![](images/50/50f3c1b8dc3b1628130d1de11abcc455fc22006f32b7486f658c8134fcc3221c.svg) from ${\\color{red}{5}},$ we multiply ${\\color{red}{5}}$ by $4$ as well: \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\times 4 = \\bbox[3px,lightgray]{\\color{red}20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $20.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3539d419-9d5a-5467-8a88-7ef318329859" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8204 :tutorial/title "Finding Missing Values in Equivalent Ratios"} :step/id #uuid "4854b84c-59a0-5a98-a9c6-9b8427de8ab8" :step/math-academy-id 186682 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073738 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073447 :question/id #uuid "a453e80d-016f-5a6c-9890-705463eb1fb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-13120"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ac1e54-c949-5ffe-b377-b0e35240a13c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050924}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434316}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalent Ratios"} :step/id #uuid "55be2711-44d0-588a-899f-562ac0853236" :step/math-academy-id 186683 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075036 :tutorial/content "We can simplify ratios by dividing both numbers in the ratio by a common factor.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the following ratio: \n\n$$\n18:9\n$$\n\nThe numbers $18$ and $9$ share a common factor of $9,$ so we can divide both numbers by $9$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,18 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,9 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,2 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18:9$ is equivalent to $2:1.$ \n\nSince $2$ and $1$ have no common factors, $2:1$ is the simplest form of this ratio. We often prefer to work with ratios in their simplest form, where possible." :tutorial/id #uuid "c7ece136-e063-594d-b25d-4cf19bca7890" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8205 :tutorial/title "Expressing Ratios in Their Simplest Form"} :step/id #uuid "23872027-241e-5f61-8492-76236e405360" :step/math-academy-id 186686 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073739 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073448 :question/id #uuid "0a57949c-efc9-5403-9e7b-08346153f184" :question/math-academy-id "e-13121"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae17eb3f-f444-5ab3-82fd-6c92b97b0e41" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434317}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Ratios"} :step/id #uuid "092ffde3-3422-5950-9ca5-9b76fcb363ff" :step/math-academy-id 186687 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074639}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073740 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073449 :question/id #uuid "dd86c5a5-b8be-5d19-a477-dcf46683881f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13123"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d16202af-d3ac-54bc-9c76-25947a7a0b60" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434318}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement"} :step/id #uuid "36a366b2-6119-57ed-87f3-fd57834dc2d6" :step/math-academy-id 186690}] :activity/title "Equivalent Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073972}]}
